  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 742 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nNorthwest flow will become dominant aloft as the aforementioned \ntrough responsible for the rain over the weekend begins to exit \nthe eastern CONUS and upper ridging builds in ahead of our next \nsystem. After overcast skies and rain on Sunday, temperatures will\nrebound a bit on Monday with the building ridge to our west. Sfc \nhigh pressure over the Appalachians will keep dry NE flow in place\nand may be enough to result in clearing skies by Monday \nafternoon. A closed upper low will dig across Oklahoma and into \nthe lower MS River Valley Tuesday into Wednesday, bringing \nincreasing chances for rain and a few thunderstorms. Model trends \nover the past several days have been fairly inconsistent with this\nstorm system, with today's runs continuing to slow the system \ndown as well as pulling the sfc low farther south. Since the \ntiming and evolution of this system will have big impacts on \nrainfall amounts and any potential for strong storms, we do not \nfeel particularly confident in any specific details at this time. \nWhat we can speak to is the continued trend with a slower system, \nincreasing our confidence that impacts to Election Day should be \nminimal with only low chances for rain (15-25%) at this time. \nTemperatures will remain near or just above normal for much of the\nlong term, with highs in the 70s and lows in the 50s to lower \n60s.",
